Q: Call to a member function on non object in CodeIgniter A: <p>It isn't documented, but any of the <code>get()</code> functions (<code>get_where()</code> in your case) can return FALSE. (Learned this the hard way.) That would seem to be your problem. <code>FALSE-&gt;num_rows()</code> has no meaning as FALSE is not a object. </p>

<p>This usually happens because the query does not build correctly.
The compiled query can be examined by doing this.</p>

<pre><code>public function validate_username($username, $user_id)
{
  $this-&gt;db-&gt;select(*)
       -&gt;from('ea_user_settings')
       -&gt;join('ea_user_settings', 'ea_user_settings.id = ea_user.id', 'inner')
       -&gt;where(array('username' =&gt; $username, 'id_users &lt;&gt; ' =&gt; $user_id, 'ea_user.data' =&gt; 0));

 $compiled_select = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get_compiled_select(NULL, FALSE);
 //inspect or var_dump($compiled_select) to see what query statement was built.
</code></pre>

<p>After setting up all the join, where and other pieces of a query I have taken to checking the truthiness of <code>get()</code> before using its return. In your case something like this. Code picks up after (or instead of) the line <code>$compiled_select = $this-&gt;...</code></p>

<pre><code>  if($query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get())
  {
      return ($query-&gt;num_rows() &gt; 0) ? FALSE : TRUE;
  } 
  else 
  {
      throw new Exception("get call returned FALSE");
  }   
}
</code></pre>

<p>For me, the Exception is useful mostly during development. But you could put your call to the <code>validate_username()</code> in a try catch block if it would be useful.</p>

Q: Getting a list of events for a pod as it starts A: <p>Your <code>watch_pod_events</code> function never returns because <code>w.stream()</code> is an infinite blocking iterator — it sits there waiting for the <em>next</em> event forever. Your <code>return message</code> line after the for loop is dead code. That's also why <code>list(w.stream(...))</code> and the list comprehension hang: you're trying to exhaust an infinite stream.</p>
<p>The <code>field_selector</code> approach (your third attempt) is actually the right idea and <em>does</em> work — but <code>involvedObject.name</code> alone isn't enough. Kubernetes events can fire for the Deployment, the ReplicaSet, and the Pod, all with different names. You need to match on the pod name specifically and also filter by <code>involvedObject.kind=Pod</code> to avoid noise.</p>
<p>Here's what you want:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>from kubernetes.client import CoreV1Api

def get_last_pod_event(name: str, namespace: str) -&gt; str | None:
    v1 = get_client()
    events = v1.list_namespaced_event(
        namespace=namespace,
        field_selector=f&quot;involvedObject.kind=Pod,involvedObject.name={name}&quot;
    ).items

    if not events:
        return None

    # sort by last timestamp descending, grab the most recent
    events.sort(key=lambda e: e.last_timestamp or e.event_time or e.metadata.creation_timestamp, reverse=True)
    return events[0].message
</code></pre>
<p>The reason you need the <code>or</code> fallback chain on the sort key: depending on how the event was created, <code>last_timestamp</code> can be <code>None</code> (newer-style events use <code>event_time</code> instead). Without that fallback you'll get a TypeError on the comparison.</p>
<p>If you want to use this for your UI status endpoint, you probably want a richer return:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def get_pod_status(name: str, namespace: str) -&gt; dict:
    v1 = get_client()

    # get pod phase first
    try:
        pod = v1.read_namespaced_pod(name=name, namespace=namespace)
    except kubernetes.client.exceptions.ApiException as e:
        if e.status == 404:
            return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;not_found&quot;, &quot;message&quot;: None}
        raise

    # get latest event for context
    events = v1.list_namespaced_event(
        namespace=namespace,
        field_selector=f&quot;involvedObject.kind=Pod,involvedObject.name={name}&quot;
    ).items
    events.sort(
        key=lambda e: e.last_timestamp or e.event_time or e.metadata.creation_timestamp,
        reverse=True
    )
    last_event = events[0].message if events else None

    # container statuses tell you more than pod phase alone
    if pod.status.container_statuses:
        cs = pod.status.container_statuses[0].state
        if cs.running:
            return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;running&quot;, &quot;message&quot;: last_event}
        if cs.waiting:
            return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;pending&quot;, &quot;message&quot;: cs.waiting.reason}
            # reason will be things like &quot;ContainerCreating&quot;, &quot;ErrImagePull&quot;,
            # &quot;ImagePullBackOff&quot;, &quot;CrashLoopBackOff&quot; etc
        if cs.terminated:
            return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;terminated&quot;, &quot;message&quot;: cs.terminated.reason}

    return {&quot;status&quot;: pod.status.phase.lower(), &quot;message&quot;: last_event}
</code></pre>
<p><code>container_statuses[0].state.waiting.reason</code> is where the actually useful user-facing info lives — that's what gives you &quot;Pulling image...&quot;, &quot;ErrImagePull&quot;, &quot;CrashLoopBackOff&quot; etc without needing to parse events at all. The event message is nice as extra context but the waiting reason is the primary signal you want for your UI.</p>
<p>If you still want a streaming approach (e.g. for a websocket or SSE endpoint), use <code>w.stream()</code> with a <code>timeout_seconds</code> so it doesn't block forever:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>for event in w.stream(v1.list_namespaced_event, namespace=namespace, timeout_seconds=10):
    ...
</code></pre>
<p>This will yield events for 10 seconds and then the iterator terminates normally.</p>

Q: CodeIgniter model error when database is not loaded: &quot;Call to a member function get() on a non-object&quot; A: <p>You have to load the Database first</p>

<pre><code>$this-&gt;load-&gt;database();
</code></pre>

<p>So, all code:</p>

<pre><code>function get_all(){
    $this-&gt;load-&gt;database();
    $query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get('test'); //test is my table 
    return $query-&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Or</strong>, load database in your <code>__construct</code> method.</p>

<p><strong>Or</strong>, IMO, It's better to autoload database by changing <code>application/config/autoload.php</code>, example is below.</p>

<pre><code>$autoload['libraries'] = array('database','form_validation'); //form_validation is for example only
</code></pre>

Q: `pgoutput` does not send BEGIN/COMMIT for empty transactions in PostgreSQL 18 — replication slot LSN does not advance A: <p>The behavior you're hitting was introduced in PostgreSQL 15 by <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/commit/d5a9d86d8f" rel="nofollow noreferrer">commit d5a9d86</a> — <code>pgoutput</code> now postpones the BEGIN until the first actual published change arrives, and if none does, it skips COMMIT too. The whole point was to save CPU/bandwidth on empty transactions that the subscriber doesn't care about.</p>
<p>You said PG17 worked — that's likely because your heartbeat table was part of the publication in that setup (intentionally or via <code>FOR ALL TABLES</code>), or the slot was created with a different config. The skipping has been active since PG15 for transactions that touch <em>only</em> non-published tables. Worth double-checking your old PG17 publication definition.</p>
<p>Either way, the fix:</p>
<p><strong>Option 1: Add the heartbeat table to the publication</strong></p>
<p>The simplest approach. If <code>pgoutput</code> sees a DML on a published table, it sends BEGIN + the change + COMMIT, and your existing <code>txMsgsCnt</code> logic works:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>ALTER PUBLICATION my_pub ADD TABLE heartbeat;
</code></pre>
<p>Your consumer will now see these as non-empty transactions (1 INSERT message). Filter them out on the consumer side by checking the relation name, and use the commit LSN to advance the slot. This is the most reliable approach because it works with any PG version and doesn't require changing your replication stream logic.</p>
<p><strong>Option 2: Use <code>pg_logical_emit_message()</code> instead of a heartbeat table</strong></p>
<p>This function emits a logical decoding message directly into the WAL stream. With <code>transactional := true</code>, it gets wrapped in a BEGIN/COMMIT pair and shows up as a <code>Message</code> event in your stream — even if no published table was touched:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>SELECT pg_logical_emit_message(true, 'heartbeat', 'ping');
</code></pre>
<p>Your <code>PGReplicationStream</code> will receive this as a <code>LogicalDecodingMessage</code> between <code>TxBeginEvent</code>/<code>TxCommitEvent</code>. You'd need to handle this message type in your consumer code — treat it as a non-empty transaction and use its LSN for flushing.</p>
<p>The advantage over option 1 is no actual table writes, no bloat, no vacuuming. The disadvantage is you need to update your consumer to recognize the message type.</p>
<p><strong>Option 3: Flush based on keepalive LSN, not transaction boundaries</strong></p>
<p>This is the most robust long-term approach but requires rethinking your consumer. Instead of waiting for BEGIN/COMMIT and counting DMLs, periodically flush based on the last LSN received from the stream — including keepalive messages.</p>
<p><code>PGReplicationStream.getLastReceiveLSN()</code> gives you the server's current position even when no data messages are flowing. You can flush this on a timer:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>// In your polling loop, regardless of whether you got data:
LogSequenceNumber serverLsn = stream.getLastReceiveLSN();
if (serverLsn != null &amp;&amp; serverLsn.asLong() &gt; lastFlushedLsn.asLong()) {
    stream.setAppliedLSN(serverLsn);
    stream.setFlushedLSN(serverLsn);
    stream.forceUpdateStatus();
    lastFlushedLsn = serverLsn;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This way you don't depend on seeing any particular message type at all. The slot advances even during periods of pure empty transactions.</p>
<p><strong>My recommendation:</strong> Option 1 if you want a 5-minute fix. Option 3 if you want to stop fighting this class of problem permanently.</p>
